# Comparing libraries

How much progress does each implementation make, and how long does it take?
These comparisons follow individual solvers on shared problems. The curves show
the objective gap $f(\mathbf{x}) - f^\star$ against elapsed time. Compare the
time needed to reach a given accuracy as well as the accuracy ultimately reached.

The workloads use enough parameters or observations to make complete solves
usually take milliseconds to roughly a tenth of a second on the measurement
machine. Sizes differ by solver: a useful workload for L-BFGS can be excessive
for a simplex method. We choose larger problems instead of repeating tiny solves
or continuing iterations after reaching the target.

| Solver | Workload |
| --- | --- |
| Levenberg–Marquardt | 32 parameters, 4,096 residuals |
| COBYLA | 30 variables, box bounds, and an active sum constraint |
| SLSQP | 100 variables and an active nonlinear ball constraint |
| Gradient descent | 100-variable Rosenbrock |
| Nelder–Mead | 12-variable Rosenbrock |
| L-BFGS | 100-variable Rosenbrock |
| NEWUOA | 30-variable Styblinski–Tang |

Configurations match where the APIs allow it. Line searches, stopping rules,
and numerical safeguards can still produce different paths. These are
comparisons of solver effectiveness, including implementation costs, rather
than measurements of identical numerical work. For comparisons between
algorithms within Basin, see [comparing solvers](../solvers/).

## Levenberg–Marquardt

Basin {data.versions.basin} and
[levenberg-marquardt](https://docs.rs/levenberg-marquardt/0.15.0/levenberg_marquardt/)
{lmVersion} jointly fit 16 exponential response curves.
Each curve has an amplitude and a decay rate, giving 32 parameters. Each has
256 exact observations at evenly spaced times in $[0,4]$, giving 4,096 residuals.
For curve $k=0,\ldots,15$, the true parameters are
$a_k=100\,000+1\,000k$ and $b_k=-1-0.02k$. Every pair starts at
$(50\,000,-0.3)$.

Both use nalgebra {data.versions.nalgebra}, the same analytic Jacobian, column
scaling, pivoted QR, and an initial scaled step-bound factor of 100. Although
curves have separate parameters, the harness supplies a single dense Jacobian
and both libraries solve the complete dense least-squares system. This tests
the dense QR path, rather than a specialized solver that exploits the blocks.

Function-reduction, orthogonality, and scaled-radius tolerances are 30 times
`f64` machine epsilon, about $6.66 \times 10^{-15}$. Basin uses trust-region
damping and disables its absolute-gradient and unscaled-step checks. Both have
a 330-residual-call budget; Basin also has a 200-iteration safety cap.

<CompetitorFigure solver="lm" problem="exp_fit" />

Both implementations recover all 32 parameters and use seven residual and six
Jacobian evaluations in this snapshot. The curve records the best objective
seen at residual evaluations, including rejected trials. Internal damping
searches and acceptance rules still differ from the reference's MINPACK path.

## COBYLA

The constrained fixture minimizes a coupled quadratic,

$$
f(\mathbf{x})=\sum_{i=1}^{n}(x_i-1)^2
 +\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(x_{i+1}-x_i)^2,
$$

with $n=30$, $0\le x_i\le2$, and $\sum_i x_i\le0.75n$.
The unique optimum has every coordinate equal to $0.75$ and
$f^\star=n/16=1.875$. The deterministic start is
$x_i=0.3+0.4(i-1)/n$.

Basin's PRIMA implementation is compared with
[cobyla](https://docs.rs/cobyla/) {data.versions.cobyla}, derived from NLopt.
Both use an initial radius of $0.5$, a final radius of about
$7.45\times10^{-9}$, a 10,000-objective-call budget, and disabled optional
objective-change stops. Objective trials enter the main curve only when their
maximum constraint violation is at most $1.49\times10^{-8}$.

<CompetitorFigure solver="cobyla" problem="quadratic_30d" />

Both returned points pass independent accuracy and feasibility checks in this
snapshot. Read the violation plot alongside objective progress: an infeasible
trial with a lower objective is not a better constrained solution. Small
negative objective gaps permitted by the feasibility tolerance appear at the
display floor.

Constraint counts use different callback units. Basin records vector callbacks
including bounds, while the reference records scalar nonlinear callbacks and
excludes native bounds.

## SLSQP

SLSQP minimizes the same coupled quadratic with $n=100$, subject to the
nonlinear constraint

$$
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_i^2\le0.75^2.
$$

The unique optimum again has every coordinate equal to $0.75$, with
$f^\star=6.25$. This case uses the same starting-point formula as COBYLA,
analytic objective gradients, and an analytic constraint Jacobian. Normalizing
the constraint by $n$ keeps its scale independent of dimension.

Basin is compared with [slsqp](https://docs.rs/slsqp/1.0.2/slsqp/)
{data.versions.slsqp}, a Rust translation of NLopt 2.7.1, and native NLopt 2.9.1.
Basin uses Kraft's composite accuracy test at $10^{-10}$. The references use an
absolute function-change tolerance of $10^{-10}$, with their other optional
objective and step tolerances disabled. Basin has a 200-iteration safety cap;
the references have a 200-objective-evaluation cap. The objective curve uses a
constraint-violation tolerance of $10^{-8}$.

<CompetitorFigure solver="slsqp" problem="constrained_quadratic" />

All three returned points pass the independent accuracy and feasibility checks
in this snapshot, but their termination statuses report failure or roundoff
limits. Those statuses are retained in the run details. Passing an independent
solution check does not mean that the solver reported convergence. The main
curve also records best feasible trials, which can differ from the returned
point.

## Gradient descent

Basin and [argmin](https://argmin-rs.org/) {data.versions.argmin} use steepest
descent with a Moré–Thuente line search on chained Rosenbrock with 100 variables.
Both start from the alternating vector $(-1.2,1,-1.2,1,\ldots)$.
They seek $f\le10^{-10}$ with a {data.iterations}-iteration safety cap.

<CompetitorFigure solver="gd" problem="rosenbrock" />

Neither run reaches the target within the budget in this snapshot. Basin
finishes its budget sooner, while argmin reaches a smaller objective gap. Read
the time at a common objective gap as well as the endpoints: faster iterations
alone do not establish a faster solve.

## Nelder–Mead

The simplex comparison uses 12-variable chained Rosenbrock, with the same
alternating starting-point formula. Basin and argmin use identical simplex
coordinates and standard coefficients. [gomez](https://docs.rs/gomez/)
{data.versions.gomez} and NLopt construct their own simplexes with their default
coefficients.

All seek $f\le10^{-10}$. Basin, argmin, and gomez have a
{data.iterations}-iteration cap; NLopt has a
{data.iterations}-objective-evaluation cap. A trial at 20 variables made little
progress within this budget, so the published case uses a smaller dimension
that exposes useful convergence behavior.

<CompetitorFigure solver="nm" problem="rosenbrock" />

Basin, argmin, and gomez reach the target in this snapshot. NLopt approaches a
positive objective gap instead. Its different simplex initialization is part of
this outcome. A stalled curve remains in the comparison.

## L-BFGS

L-BFGS uses the same 100-variable Rosenbrock fixture and start as gradient
descent. All three retain ten curvature pairs. Basin and argmin use a Moré–Thuente
line search. NLopt supplies its bundled L-BFGS implementation, whose line search
is not configurable through the harness's API.

All seek $f\le10^{-10}$. Basin and argmin have a
{data.iterations}-iteration safety cap, with argmin's gradient and cost-change
tolerances zeroed. NLopt has a {data.iterations}-objective-evaluation cap.
These limits bound the runs; they do not prescribe equal work.

<CompetitorFigure solver="lbfgs" problem="rosenbrock" />

All three reach the target in this snapshot. Increasing the dimension makes the
cost of curvature history and the optimization path more visible than in the
previous two-variable fixture. These cheap analytic evaluations still expose
solver overhead more strongly than an expensive application objective would.

## NEWUOA

Basin and NLopt run Powell's model-based derivative-free method on
30-dimensional Styblinski–Tang, starting at the origin. Basin follows the
modernized PRIMA implementation, while NLopt uses Powell's reference.

The initial radius is $0.5$, the final radius is $10^{-6}$, and the interpolation
set has $2n+1$ points. Basin has a 10,000-iteration safety cap and NLopt a
10,000-evaluation cap. Both stop on their radius criteria before those limits.

<CompetitorFigure solver="newuoa" problem="styblinski" />

Both implementations approach the known global minimum from this start. This
case exercises larger interpolation models, but a single start does not measure
the probability of finding the global minimum on a multimodal problem.

## Methodology and reproduction

Measured {data.generatedAt} on {data.env.cpu} ({data.env.os}/{data.env.arch}),
using {data.env.compiler}. Serial runs use {data.env.threads} thread. LM uses
nalgebra {data.versions.nalgebra}; other cases use `Vec<f64>`, except for
gomez's bundled `nalgebra::DVector<f64>`. Native NLopt is accessed through the
Rust `nlopt` bindings, version {data.versions.nlopt}.

Each case warms up once. Each timestamp is the median at the corresponding
sample over {data.repetitions} repetitions of the same deterministic run.
The harness checks that numerical traces agree across repetitions before
combining timestamps. Repetitions reduce timing noise; they are not independent
starting points or a measure of reliability across problems.

Timings include initialization and trace instrumentation. Some driving loops
construct their executor before starting the clock. LM and COBYLA also include
solver teardown; NLopt curves include an explicit endpoint after optimization.
Independent solution checks and constrained-trace reconstruction happen outside
the timer. Argmin uses an observer, Basin and gomez use driving loops, and NLopt
uses objective callbacks, so sampling and instrumentation costs differ.

The objective curves retain at most about 512 samples plus the endpoint from
each full recorded trace. Feasibility plots retain all recorded trials. Objective gaps are floored at $10^{-16}$ for display,
including small negative gaps allowed by constrained feasibility tolerances.
Absolute times depend on the machine. Results use source revision
<code>{data.env.revision.slice(0, 7)}</code>{revisionNote}.

From the repository root:

```sh
mkdir -p target
cargo run -p competitor-bench --release --bin trace > target/competitor-traces.json
pnpm --dir web collect:competitors
```

The [trace harness](https://github.com/jolars/basin/blob/main/crates/competitor-bench/src/bin/trace.rs)
and its shared fixtures define the problems and settings. The
[collection script](https://github.com/jolars/basin/blob/main/web/scripts/collect-competitors.ts)
validates the results and adds machine and version metadata before updating
the committed figures.
